1.3 Bob Jenkins, lookup3

Yices uses hash functions from Bob Jenkins' lookup3 code. The implementations
are in src/utils/hash_functions.c and src/utils/hash_functions.h, and derived
hash codes appear in a number of other files, including
src/solvers/egraph/composites.c, src/utils/cache.c,
src/utils/pair_hash_map.c, src/utils/pair_hash_sets.c, src/utils/ptr_sets.c,
src/solvers/bv/bvsolver.c, src/solvers/simplex/arith_atomtable.c, and
src/solvers/egraph/egraph_eq_stats.c.

Bob Jenkins placed lookup3 in the public domain. The Yices sources record this
where the code is used, and give http://www.burtleburtle.net as the source.

1.4 Timsort

src/utils/stable_sort.h and src/utils/stable_sort.c implement a stable sort.
The header records that the implementation is based on Tim Peters' timsort, as
used by Python, with the galloping part omitted, and cites two references: the
timsort description at http://bugs.python.org/file4451/timsort.txt and the
CPython file Objects/listobject.c.

Repository history establishes when the Yices implementation entered this
repository, but not that every source expression was written independently.
The files were authored in October 2013 and then evolved through Yices-specific
changes, including a low-memory merge and a later correction to the run-stack
invariant. Yices omits CPython's galloping implementation.

One compact routine does overlap. The five executable statements in Yices'
`min_run` function follow the same order and operations as CPython's
`merge_compute_minrun` in both CPython 3.3.2 and 3.4.0a3. Yices changes the
type to `uint32_t`, uses an unsigned `1u` literal, and omits CPython's
assertion and inline comment. The repository evidence does not establish
whether this sequence was copied or independently re-expressed from the
published algorithm. Because the Yices header expressly says the
implementation is based on Python's timsort, this release conservatively
treats that sequence as CPython-derived material.

The exact 254-line CPython 3.4.0a3 LICENSE file accompanies this notice as
LICENSES/CPython-3.4.0a3-LICENSE.txt. Its SHA-256 is
be4ab04487306fe817abdd569a7a64e3a727d4e176114c956dca25df71bb7fec.
That file retains the Python Software Foundation License Version 2 and the
copyright notice it requires. Brief summary of changes: Yices adapted the
minimum-run computation as stated above, integrated it into a separate
pointer-array stable sort, omitted galloping, and subsequently changed its
merge buffering and run-stack handling.


