Section 3. Components incorporated into the binary distributions
-----------------------------------------------------------------

Yices binary distributions are produced by compiling Yices and linking it with
the libraries below. Some are linked statically, meaning the library's code is
copied into the file that is distributed. Some are linked dynamically, meaning
the distributed file requires a copy of the library on the system where it
runs.

The tables below give the placement in each published binary archive.
Placement was determined by inspecting the distributed files themselves, not
from build configuration.

The paths in this file are of three kinds. A path such as bin/yices,
lib/libyices.a or LICENSES/MIT.txt names a file inside a published binary or
source archive, written relative to the top of that archive. A path such as
src/utils/hash_functions.c or contrib/craigtracer.cpp records where code came
from in a source tree, and is not a claim that the file accompanies any
distribution. A path beginning with a slash names a file on the reader's own
system.

The relink kits are a further case. They carry LICENSE, NOTICES and a licenses
directory, spelled in lower case there, and they carry no PROVENANCE. The
three example paths of the first kind given above do not resolve inside a kit.

An installed copy is another such case. Section 4 gives its layout. The
license texts are not in a directory named LICENSES there, so a path of the
first kind such as LICENSES/MIT.txt does not resolve inside it.

