Section 5. Component and version record
---------------------------------------

The fixed component versions given above are GMP 6.3.0, CUDD 3.0.0 and CaDiCaL
3.0.0. LibPoly, Kissat and CryptoMiniSat are also fixed for this release, by
source revision rather than by a version number, and this file does not give
those revisions.

Component versions that depend on the machine that produced a given binary
archive are not written here, because writing them would make this file wrong
for any archive built elsewhere. They are recorded in the PROVENANCE file that
the binary archives carry. That file names the compiler the build used and
records the first line of its version output. It identifies the C library the
binaries were built against. Where the build links a C library archive, it
gives that archive's checksum and names the source package the archive came
from. It also gives the GMP version and the file name and checksum of the GMP
source asset. On Linux it also records the local name and immutable ID of the
derived builder image and the pinned base image separately. All three
builder-image fields say that they are not applicable in a native macOS build.

Each platform archive carries the manual PDF built and validated on that
platform. PROVENANCE records that exact PDF's checksum. TeX implementations
can produce different PDF layout bytes from the same manual source, so the
Linux, Intel macOS, and Apple Silicon checksums need not match. The release
workflow extracts text from every PDF and applies the same required-content
and stale-content gates to all three.

PROVENANCE, YICES-SOURCE-RECEIPT, and the platform validation receipts name
and hash some build receipts, manifests, and validation scripts that are
internal release records rather than publication assets. Those hashes bind
the inputs used by the release workflow and support the release operator's
audit. They are not an assertion that a recipient can obtain or independently
authenticate every named support file from the public asset set.

LibPoly's source is prepared once, before any platform build, rather than
reconstructed afterward on each builder. Every builder verifies and compiles
the same canonical archive. PROVENANCE records its repository, commit, tree,
file name and checksum, the checksums of the normal and PIC archives used by
that build, and the file name and checksum of the canonical RELEASE-INPUTS
manifest. The final collector checks those fields in every binary archive and
relink kit against the manifest before it copies any asset. The release
SHA256SUMS then verifies the published source archive and manifest relative to
a checksum obtained through a trusted release channel. The checksum file by
itself does not authenticate its publisher.

Section 6. Materials for rebuilding
------------------------------------

Some of the libraries built into the Yices binaries are distributed under
terms that give you the right to modify them and rebuild Yices against your
modified version. The material needed to do that is published as a separate
asset alongside the binary archives, together with the corresponding library
source.

The platform relink kits include REBUILDING.md and a private-prefix GMP/LibPoly
build recipe. The Linux kit additionally includes helpers to prepare the exact
patched glibc SRPM source and explicitly link the application objects with a
recipient-built glibc and its startup objects. These use ordinary compatible
toolchain prerequisites and do not install a C runtime into the host system.
They substantiate a source/application-object route, not a promise of bitwise
reproduction or legal sign-off. Every binary recipient must be given access to
the matching required source and relink material, even when only the smaller
binary archive is downloaded.

The release asset set separately publishes the exact six dependency source
archives named and hashed in RELEASE-INPUTS: GMP, LibPoly, CUDD, CaDiCaL,
Kissat and CryptoMiniSat. Those archives preserve the upstream notices and
license files of their retained material; section 3.9 describes the explicitly
reduced CryptoMiniSat export. Section 4 identifies the license texts accompanying
the release, including GPL-2.0-only for the files in the CryptoMiniSat source
asset.

You do not need any of it to install or run Yices. If you only want to use
Yices, the ordinary distribution archive is complete on its own.
